lisa-design:
- Scope split BCH-1316 vs BCH-1330: cedar is OPT-IN (CCF_AUTHZ_DRIVER=cedar); the DEFAULT stays `builtin` so this PR is zero-behavior-change. Flipping the default to cedar is explicitly BCH-1330 — do NOT change DefaultAuthzDriver here.
- Bundled role policies are GENERATED from the manifest roles: block at startup (CompileRolePolicies/renderRolePolicies), not shipped as a static .cedar file. The manifest is the single source of truth; editing roles means editing manifest.yaml. Output is deterministic (sorted) for golden tests.
- Cedar RBAC is deny-by-default: a subject with no assigned role is denied everything. This DIFFERS from builtin (authenticated=allowed) but only applies when cedar is selected — intentional, it is real RBAC.
- Group-based assignment is wired but INERT until BCH-1328 populates subject.Props["groups"] (native ∪ SSO groups). User-based (by email) assignment is fully live now. Deliberately did NOT build an SSO/DB groups resolver here — that surface is BCH-1328's responsibility; subjectGroups() reads an existing prop so it goes live additively.
- No pluggable PIP interface was introduced. The ticket says not to pre-design one; the C0 bundled policies reference only role×action×resource.type, so no C1/C2 resolvers fire. Don't add a PIP abstraction without a concrete consumer.
- Entity model: principal entity (CCF::User / CCF::Agent, id = subject.ID/email) is made a member (Cedar `in`) of CCF::Role::"<role>" entities built per-request from static assignments. Resources are CCF::<PascalCase> using the SAME cedarEntityName mapping + CCF namespace as `ccf authz export --format=cedar`, so bundled policies, exported schema and operator .cedar files all name the same entities.
- Agents get the `agent` role by default (zero-config ingest), overridable via authz-roles.yaml `agents:`. normalize() defaults empty -> "agent".
- Operator .cedar files (CCF_AUTHZ_CEDAR_POLICY_DIR) AUGMENT the bundled roles (added under op:<file>:<id> ids); they don't replace them. Cedar forbid>permit lets operators carve exceptions, incl. out of admin.
- Startup validation: role names in authz-roles.yaml are validated against the manifest (unknown -> fatal); missing assignment file is tolerated (users denied, agents still work, warn); malformed/strict-unknown-key file -> fatal. Fail-fast over silently denying everyone.
- Dependency: github.com/cedar-policy/cedar-go v1.8.0 (+ golang.org/x/exp transitive). cedar.Authorize is a pure function (no DB/network) so the cedar driver never returns ErrUnavailable and fail_mode never affects it.
